5. Technical Architecture
5.1 Architecture Pattern
The Uplift Lab begins as a well-structured modular monolith with bounded contexts per module. This approach gives us the development velocity of a monolith with the conceptual clarity of microservices. Each module has its own domain models, business logic, and API surface, but they are deployed as a single application with clear seams for future extraction.

Why modular monolith over microservices from day one?

Team size reality: Early-stage teams (5-10 engineers) cannot effectively operate a distributed system. Monolith reduces operational overhead.
Faster iteration: Cross-module features (e.g., "complete a financial literacy course and get a mentor recommendation") are trivial in a monolith, complex across services.
Clear extraction path: Bounded contexts with event-driven communication create natural seams. When a module needs independent scaling (e.g., Health module under heavy telehealth load), it can be extracted to its own service without rewriting business logic.
