7. Data Ethics Framework
The Uplift Lab handles sensitive data across health, finance, legal, and personal domains for communities that have been historically exploited by data systems. Our data ethics framework is not an afterthought — it is foundational architecture, informed by the Urban Institute's Principles for Advancing Equitable Data Practice, the World Economic Forum's Data Equity Framework, and Brookings' community-led data governance model.

7.1 Data Sovereignty
Community members own their data. The platform is a steward, not an owner. Users can export all their data at any time in machine-readable formats (JSON, CSV).
Plain-language consent. No legal jargon. Every data permission is explained in clear, conversational language with concrete examples of what it means.
Right to deletion. Users can delete their account and all associated data. Deletion is real — not a soft-delete hidden behind a "deactivation" label. 30-day grace period for recovery, then permanent, irreversible deletion.
Data portability. Users can transfer their data to other platforms or services. No vendor lock-in. Export formats follow open standards (FHIR for health, FDX for financial, standard CSV for everything else).
7.2 Consent Architecture
Granular, opt-in consent per module and per data type. Users consent separately to sharing health data, financial data, location data, etc. No all-or-nothing consent bundles.
Visual consent dashboard. A single screen where users see exactly what data is collected, which modules access it, which third parties (if any) receive it, and a one-tap revoke button for each permission.
Consent versioning. All consent actions are timestamped and versioned. If a data policy changes, users are re-prompted and old consent does not carry forward.
Minor protections. Enhanced consent requirements for users under 18. Parent/guardian consent workflows. Limited data collection for minors.
7.3 Bias Monitoring
Algorithmic fairness audits on all AI/ML features — quarterly internal reviews, annual external audits by an independent firm.
External Bias Review Board: 5-7 members including data scientists, ethicists, community advocates, and affected community members. Meets quarterly, publishes findings publicly.
Transparent model cards for all production AI systems, documenting: training data, intended use, limitations, performance metrics disaggregated by demographics, and known biases.
User feedback loops: Users can flag AI recommendations as inappropriate, biased, or unhelpful. Flags are reviewed by the fairness team and feed into model retraining.
7.4 Community Data Governance Council
Structure: 7-9 elected community representatives covering all six module domains, plus an at-large youth representative and an elder representative.

Authority: Reviews and approves all new data uses, data partnerships, and changes to data policy. Has binding veto power over data partnerships that the community deems exploitative or misaligned.

Cadence: Meets monthly. Quarterly public reports on data governance decisions. Annual community-wide data governance town hall.

Compensation: Council members are compensated for their time. This is labor, not volunteerism.

7.5 Research Ethics
Any research using platform data must be community-approved by the Data Governance Council and IRB-reviewed by an independent institutional review board.
Community-first results sharing: Research findings must be shared with the community before publication. Community has a 30-day review period to provide context and flag concerns.
No data sales. Period. Platform data is never sold to third parties, advertisers, data brokers, or researchers. Research access is granted via controlled environments with de-identified data only.
Reciprocity requirement: Any researcher using platform data must demonstrate tangible benefit to the community — not just academic output.
8. Governance Model
8.1 Organizational Structure
The Uplift Lab is structured as a Public Benefit Corporation (PBC) with a path to cooperative ownership. Unlike traditional VC-backed startups, the PBC charter legally binds the organization to its stated public benefit mission. Community ownership is not aspirational — it is structural.

Year 1-3: Public Benefit Corporation with mission lock in charter. Community Advisory Board with binding input.
Year 3-5: Transition to a platform cooperative model where community members, employees, and institutional partners hold governance stakes.
Anti-acquisition clause: Charter includes provisions preventing acquisition by entities that would compromise the mission. Community vote required for any change of control.
