13.3 Hiring Philosophy
Build With, Not For: The Uplift Lab is committed to hiring from the community it serves. This is not performative diversity — it is a core design principle. People who understand the problems from lived experience build better solutions than those who study the problems from a distance.

Target 60%+ team members from affected communities across all roles — engineering, design, product, community, content, and leadership.
Invest in pipeline: Partner with HBCUs, coding bootcamps serving Black students (Kura Labs, /dev/color, Code2040), and community colleges for junior engineering roles with structured mentorship.
Compensate fairly: Market-rate salaries benchmarked to cost-of-living. No "mission discount" — exploitation of workers' passion for the mission undermines the mission itself.
Remote-first: Distributed team structure enables hiring from any geography, particularly communities most affected by the issues the platform addresses.
Apprenticeship program: Structured apprenticeship pathway for community members transitioning into tech careers. Apprentices contribute to the platform while earning credentials through Uplift Learn.
14. Risk Register
Risk	Likelihood	Impact	Mitigation Strategy
Data Breach / Security Incident	Medium	Critical	Zero-trust architecture. Encryption at rest and in transit. SOC 2 compliance. Regular penetration testing. Incident response plan with 24-hour community notification commitment. Cyber insurance.
Community Trust Erosion	Medium	Critical	Community governance with binding authority. Transparent data practices. Public audit reports. Community Data Governance Council veto power. Any trust violation triggers mandatory community town hall within 72 hours.
Funding Gaps Between Phases	High	High	Diversified funding portfolio (grants + government + SaaS + corporate). 6-month operating reserve target. Bridge funding relationships with 2-3 foundations. Revenue generation beginning in Phase 1 via early institutional partners.
Scope Creep	High	Medium	Bounded contexts enforce module boundaries. Community Advisory Board prioritizes roadmap. Strict MVP discipline per module. Feature requests tracked and prioritized against KPIs, not loudest voice.
Integration Failures	Medium	Medium	API contracts tested in CI/CD. Fallback UX for every integration (manual entry, cached data). Integration health monitoring dashboard. Vendor relationship management for critical APIs (Plaid, Doxy.me).
AI Bias Incidents	Medium	High	Bias monitoring infrastructure built from day one. External Bias Review Board. Model cards for all AI systems. User feedback loops. Ability to disable AI recommendations per user. Mandatory fairness audits before any AI feature launches.
Regulatory Changes	Medium	Medium	Legal counsel on retainer for health (HIPAA), finance (fintech regulation), and data privacy (state privacy laws). Modular compliance architecture — each module can adapt independently. Policy monitoring via legislative tracking tools.
Team Burnout	High	Medium	Sustainable pace policies: no crunch culture, flexible schedules, mental health benefits, mission-aligned but not mission-martyred. Regular team retrospectives. Burnout monitoring. Adequate staffing per phase — do not under-resource.
Community Engagement Fatigue	Medium	Medium	Notification fatigue management (preference-driven, frequency caps). Module onboarding that delivers value immediately (not weeks of setup). Celebrate wins visibly. Rotate community advisory responsibilities. Compensate all community labor.
Open-Source Free Rider Problem	Medium	Low	AGPL license requires derivative works to be open-sourced. Managed SaaS offering provides revenue regardless of self-hosted deployments. Community contributions improve the core. This is a feature, not a bug.
Multi-City Deployment Complexity	Medium	Medium	Multi-tenant architecture designed from Phase 0. City-specific configuration layer (resources, integrations, branding). Deployment playbook and onboarding process documented and tested with first 3 cities before broader rollout.
15. Appendices
Appendix A: Glossary of Terms
Term	Definition
AGPL	Affero General Public License — an open-source license that requires derivative works (including SaaS deployments) to make source code available.
Bounded Context	A domain-driven design concept where a module has its own models, logic, and language, decoupled from other modules.
CDFI	Community Development Financial Institution — a financial institution certified by the U.S. Treasury that provides credit and financial services to underserved markets.
CHW	Community Health Worker — a frontline public health worker who is a trusted member of the community they serve.
FDX	Financial Data Exchange — a nonprofit organization operating a common, interoperable standard for secure access to user-permissioned financial data.
FHIR	Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources — a standard for exchanging healthcare data electronically, developed by HL7.
GAD-7	Generalized Anxiety Disorder 7-item scale — a validated screening tool for anxiety.
HBCU	Historically Black College or University.
LTI	Learning Tools Interoperability — a standard developed by IMS Global for integrating learning applications with educational platforms.
Modular Monolith	A software architecture that structures a single application as independent modules with clear boundaries, providing a path to microservices.
mTLS	Mutual Transport Layer Security — a protocol where both client and server authenticate each other.
PBC	Public Benefit Corporation — a corporate form that legally binds the organization to pursue both profit and a stated public benefit.
PHQ-9	Patient Health Questionnaire-9 — a validated screening tool for depression.
PWA	Progressive Web App — a web application that uses modern web technologies to deliver app-like experiences.
RBAC	Role-Based Access Control — a method of restricting system access based on the roles of individual users.
VITA	Volunteer Income Tax Assistance — an IRS program offering free tax preparation to low-to-moderate-income individuals.
